# tail - show the last lines (or bytes) of files, or follow a log

## SYNOPSIS
    tail [-n N | -N | -n +N | +N] [-c N] [-f] [-qv] [file | pattern | - ...]

## DESCRIPTION
Shows the end of each file: the last 10 lines, unless told otherwise.
Good for the newest entries of a log, or the end of a long output.

A file is read from its end backwards, so the last lines of even a very
big file come at once. The input (a pipe) is read through, keeping the
last lines. When several files are shown, each comes under a line
`==> name <==`.

With **-f** tail keeps watching the files after showing their end, and
shows whatever is added to them, until you press Ctrl-C: ideal for
watching a log file grow.

## OPTIONS
- **-n** N
  the last N lines.
- **-N** the same, shorter: `tail -20` is `tail -n 20`.
- **-n** +N
  everything from line N on (`-n +2` leaves out the first line).
- **+N** the same, the old way: `tail +2 file`.
- **-c** N
  the last N bytes instead of lines; `-c +N` from byte N on. N may end
  in k or M (1024s).
- **-f** follow: wait for more to be added and show it, until Ctrl-C.
  The files are looked at four times a second. **-F** is the same.
- **-q** never show the file names.
- **-v** always show the file names, even for one file.

## EXAMPLES
    tail S:Startup-Sequence
    tail -n 50 RAM:debug.log
    tail -f RAM:debug.log
    ls -lt Work:Downloads | tail -3
    tail -n +2 Work:Data/table.csv | sort

## RETURN CODES
0 fine (also when **-f** is ended with Ctrl-C), 5 a file could not be
read or a pattern matched nothing, 10 bad arguments, 20 stopped with
Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
With **-f**, a file that gets shorter (started anew) is followed from
its start again. **-f** follows only real files, not the input, and at
most 32 of them.

Patterns may be written `#?` or `\*`; upper and lower case are the same.

`tail ?` shows the usage line.

